3	== Specification/appendices/changes.tex:2614-2740
4	
5	\subsection{Traits with comprises clauses read at the level of values}
6	\seclabel{revival-covering}
7	
8	\begin{description}
9	\item[Affected sections] \secref{internal-types};
10	\secref{abstractFunctionDeclarations}; a draft note in the subsection ``Export
11	Statements'' of \secref{components}; the effect of \secref{revival-comprises}.
12	\item[Change] \secref{internal-types} defines covering: a type covers another
13	when every value of the second is a value of the first; a type covers each of
14	its subtypes, and a trait with a \KWD{comprises} clause is covered by the union
15	of its listed types; covering is not the subtype relation.
16	Its example concludes that $V$ covers $S \inter T$, where it concluded that
17	$V = S \inter T$, and a sentence says that a trait may stand between $S$ or $T$
18	and their listed types, so that $S \inter T$ is not in general equal to a named
19	type.
20	\secref{abstractFunctionDeclarations} asks the argument types of the concrete
21	declarations to cover the argument type of the abstract declaration, where it
22	asked their union to equal it, and, in its example, for a definition for every
23	type listed in the \KWD{comprises} clause of \TYP{Molecule} or definitions that
24	together cover those types, where it asked for one for every immediate subtype.
25	Each of these four passages has a box.
26	The draft note says ``all its listed types'' where it said ``all its immediate
27	subtraits''; a draft note prints only in a draft build, so it has no box.
28	The effect of \secref{revival-comprises} no longer says that these passages are
29	not revised.
30	A correction of 9~October 2026 changes the last sentence of the box after the
31	example in \secref{abstractFunctionDeclarations} and the sentence of this
32	entry's effect that repeats it.
33	They said that the compiled type checker checks that an object's concrete
34	methods cover the abstract methods it inherits, ``reading no \KWD{comprises}
35	clause''; they now say that it reads such clauses to do so, so that concrete
36	methods over the types that a trait lists together cover an abstract method
37	over that trait.
38	The normative text is unchanged.
39	\item[Rationale] This change does not follow from route~A; the revival decided
40	on 29~September 2026 that the passages that read a \KWD{comprises} clause at the
41	level of types are restated at the level of values, which is how
42	\secref{trait-decls} reads the clause since its revision of 28~September 2026
43	(\secref{revival-comprises}).
44	The team's later Types chapter names covering beside subtyping and exclusion as
45	a relation between types, and says that an instantiation of a trait with a
46	\KWD{comprises} clause ``is covered by the union'' of the listed types
47	(\nolinkurl{Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick}, lines 36--38
48	and 384--390, in the revival's repository).
49	Welterweight Fortress, the type group's calculus of 2012, reads the clause at
50	the level of values and counts an abstract declaration as covered when it is
51	implemented for all the types comprised
52	(\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/grammar.tick}, lines 21--22;
53	\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/fig-covered.tick}).
54	Under that reading a trait with static parameters may stand unlisted between a
55	trait with a \KWD{comprises} clause and its listed types; an instantiation of it
56	is then a subtype of the closed trait and of no listed type, though every value
57	of it is a value of a listed type, so the passages' conclusions hold of values
58	and not of types.
59	The full reasoning is in
60	\nolinkurl{explorations/compile-ladder/rung-spec-overloading/decision-record.md}
61	and \nolinkurl{explorations/reviews/comprises-type-level-judgement.md} in the
62	revival's repository.
63	The sentence that the correction of 9~October 2026 changes was false when it was
64	written: since 6~June 2012 the team's compiled type checker, testing whether an
65	abstract method's parameter type is a subtype of the union of the parameter
66	types of the concrete methods below it, tests whether the union of the types
67	that a trait with a \KWD{comprises} clause lists is a subtype of that union
68	(\nolinkurl{ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/typechecker/AbstractMethodChecker.scala}
69	and \nolinkurl{ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/types/TypeAnalyzer.scala},
70	in the revival's repository).
71	It accepts an object whose methods over the two types that a trait lists cover
72	an abstract method over that trait, and refuses the same object when the trait
73	has no \KWD{comprises} clause.
74	The full reasoning is in
75	\nolinkurl{explorations/compile-ladder/rung-walk-override/REPORT.md} in the
76	revival's repository.
77	\item[Effect] No example of this specification becomes invalid.
78	Covering is not subtyping: where an instantiation of a trait with static
79	parameters stands between $S$ and $T$ and their listed types, its value is a
80	value of $V$ but its static type is not a subtype of $V$, and the compiled type
81	checker refuses to assign it to a variable of type $V$; the interpreter, which
82	has no static types, runs such an assignment.
83	The compiled type checker checks, at each object declaration, that the
84	object's concrete methods cover the abstract methods it inherits, and reads
85	\KWD{comprises} clauses to do so: concrete methods over the types that a trait
86	lists together cover an abstract method over that trait; top-level abstract
87	function declarations run on neither implementation.
88	\item[Original text] \nolinkurl{Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/types-vals-vars.tex},
89	lines 568--570:
90	\begin{quote}
91	because of the \KWD{comprises} clauses of $S$ and $T$ and the \KWD{excludes}
92	clause of $U$, any subtype of both $S$ and $T$ must be a subtype of $V$.  Thus,
93	$V = S \inter T$.
94	\end{quote}
95	\nolinkurl{Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/functions.tex}, lines 377--378:
96	\begin{quote}
97	Furthermore, the union of the argument types of the concrete declarations for
98	\VAR{f} must be equal to \VAR{T}.
99	\end{quote}
100	and lines 413--415:
101	\begin{quote}
102	For the latter, the programmer must provide a definition for every immediate
103	subtype of \TYP{Molecule}, or it is a static error.
104	\end{quote}
105	\nolinkurl{Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/components/source-code.tex}, line
106	429, the words of the draft note's second item that changed:
107	\begin{quote}
108	provided that all its immediate subtraits are considered to have concrete
109	method declaration with that signature.
110	\end{quote}
111	The effect of \secref{revival-comprises}, the revival's text of 28~September
112	2026, ended:
113	\begin{quote}
114	The passages that read a \KWD{comprises} clause at the level of types, the
115	intersection of two traits with such clauses (\secref{internal-types},
116	\secref{more-specific-rule}) and the definitions an abstract function
117	declaration requires (\secref{abstractFunctionDeclarations}), are not revised;
118	whether they are to be restated at the level of values is not settled.
119	\end{quote}
120	The box after the example in \secref{abstractFunctionDeclarations}, the
121	revival's text of 30~September 2026, ended:
122	\begin{quote}
123	The compiled type checker checks, at each object declaration, that the
124	object's concrete methods cover the abstract methods it inherits, reading no
125	\KWD{comprises} clause; top-level abstract function declarations run on neither
126	implementation of the revival.
127	\end{quote}
128	and the effect of this entry said the same in its words.
129	\item[Route C] The same under route~C: the reading of a \KWD{comprises} clause
130	at the level of values does not depend on the rule on instantiations.
131	\end{description}
